JWasm: -Zj, Masm 5 code generation

Masm 5 is a two-pass assembler that fixes the size of every instruction in
pass one and never shrinks it.  -Zj makes JWasm generate the same code:

 - a forward JMP reserves 3 bytes; a forward reference in the second
   operand of an instruction with a register destination is a memory
   operand; a forward referenced variable is a WORD (the 80/83 group keeps
   the short form if the immediate fits in a byte); later passes pad an
   instruction that turned out shorter with NOPs (EB xx 90, ...)
 - a forward reference in a displacement keeps the 16-bit displacement
 - AX, imm uses the accumulator form, not opcode 83
 - EVEN/ALIGN pad with 90h in a segment that contains instructions, else
   with zeros, and always write the padding
 - a structure name that is an operand of + or - is 0;
   number + struct field has the type of the field
 - fixups always have an explicit frame (no F5 "frame of target")

This is what GR-DOS needs to reproduce the MS-DOS 4.00 binaries built
with MASM 5.10 byte for byte; see docs/m2-jwasm.md.

